FINAL ANSWER:
**Short answer:** the person most of this project depends on is **Troy Goode**. He wrote most of the core library (`lib/index.js`) and most of the tests. One caveat matters: he only counts as "active" if two recent commits from `troy@laptop.example` are really his. The tool guesses they are, but nobody has confirmed it.

All numbers come from `surviving-lines`, which counts the lines each person wrote that are still in the code (using git blame). I used the pre-computed brief plus `--group-by dir`, `--files` and `--without "Troy Goode"`. Here, **bus factor** means the smallest number of people whose surviving lines add up to 50% of the code.

## Whole repository (1,662 non-blank lines; 1,985 counting blank lines)

| Owner | Non-blank lines | Counting blank lines | Commits |
|---|---|---|---|
| Troy Goode (2 addresses, merge is a guess) | 649 (39.0%) | — | 95 |
| ↳ <email redacted> only | 605 (36.4%) | 762 (38.4%) | 93 (29.5%) |
| Douglas Christopher Wilson | 551 (33.2%) | 629 (31.7%) | 143 (45.4%) |
| Íñigo Marquínez Prado | 80 (4.8%) | 95 (4.8%) | 2 |
| Jon Church | 59 (3.5%) | 72 (3.6%) | 3 |
| Sebastian Beltran | 57 (3.4%) | 67 (3.4%) | 2 |
| Ulises Gascón | 46 (2.8%) | 52 (2.6%) | 3 |
| 39 more people | 264 (15.9%) | 308 (15.5%) | |

- **Bus factor: 2** (Troy Goode and Douglas Christopher Wilson), on both line counts. It takes 5 people to reach 80%.
- **Counting only active people: 1, Troy Goode.** "Active" means at least one commit in the 365 days before 2026-04-12, the date of the latest commit (not today). This figure only uses the lines of those 9 active people.
- **Activity:** 9 of 44 people committed in that window and hold 51.7% of the lines. The other 48.3% belongs to people with no commits then; the largest is Douglas Christopher Wilson at 33.2%.

## Areas that depend on one person

| Area | Bus factor | Who | Finding |
|---|---|---|---|
| `lib/` (the core, 214 lines) | 1 | Troy Goode, 144 lines (67.3%) | **Single point of failure** |
| `test/` (820 lines) | 1 | Troy Goode, 420 (51.2%); Douglas Christopher Wilson 344 (42.0%) | Single point of failure (he is the only active owner) |
| Root files (323 lines) | 2; 1 among active people | Douglas Christopher Wilson 95 (29.4%), Troy Goode 85 (26.3%) | Single point of failure (Troy is the only active owner) |
| `.github/` (305 lines) | 2; 2 among active people | Douglas Christopher Wilson 92 (30.2%), Íñigo Marquínez Prado 80 (26.2%) | **Orphaned**: the top owners are gone. Sebastian Beltran and Ulises Gascón are the active ones. |

"Single point of failure" and "orphaned" are separate findings, not two levels of the same risk.

**Files where one person holds 90% or more** (at least 20 lines):
- `.github/workflows/scorecard.yml`: Íñigo Marquínez Prado, 98.4%, inactive
- `.github/workflows/codeql.yml`: Sebastian Beltran, 100%
- `test/vary.js`: Troy Goode, 100%

`LICENSE` is also at 94.4% (Douglas Christopher Wilson) but is under 20 lines.

**Orphaned files** (top owner inactive, and no active person holds 20%): 6 of 18, which is 7.8% of the lines. The main ones are `scorecard.yml`, `HISTORY.md` and `LICENSE`.

## If Troy Goode left
- He holds 649 lines (39.0%) and is the top owner of 6 files.
- Two files would have nobody left who knows them: `lib/index.js` (the best remaining owner is Douglas Christopher Wilson at 9.3%) and `test/vary.js` (nobody else has any lines).
- The bus factor would drop from 2 to 1, counted on the remaining lines. Douglas Christopher Wilson would inherit the most: 399 lines in 8 files.
- This counts lines, not who actually understands the code.

## Who to ask
- **Troy Goode** is the main source of knowledge (39.0% of the lines). His last commit from the gmail address was in 2019, though.
- **Douglas Christopher Wilson** is second (33.2%) and has had no commits since 2021.
- For `.github/`, the practical contacts are **Sebastian Beltran** and **Ulises Gascón**, who are both active.

## Caveats
- **Troy's "active" status rests on a guess.** It comes only from two commits by `troy@laptop.example` on 2026-04-10, which hold 44 lines. The tool calls the match strong, but it is based on the name alone and isn't confirmed. If that isn't him, `lib/` has no active owner at all and is orphaned rather than a single point of failure. Can you confirm whether it's the same person?
- **The latest commit was set aside.** Commit 62628c0, by "Fiona Formatter", only reformatted code: it rewrote 1,016 lines in 4 files. Its lines are credited back to the people who wrote them. Without that correction, Troy has 554 lines (33.3%) and Douglas has 376 (22.6%), and the bus factor is still 2. Seven more commits looked mechanical but were kept in the counts, including 739dc11 "docs: reformat license".
- **Other duplicate names:** Alejandro Caravaca Puchades also appears as `alex94puchades` (same email, 30 lines) and the tool counts them as one person. I haven't written a `.mailmap` file.
- **Left out:** the dependabot bot (10 lines, 11 commits). Lines in commits that GitHub made for someone else (19 commits) are credited to the actual authors.
- **History:** it starts on 2013-01-30 with an imported commit covering 44% of the files. The table covers work from that point on, so older lines go to whoever did the import.
- Line counts don't measure review work, design, or who understands the code.

